@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+Client-side performance monitoring
+==================================
+
+`performance-monitor.js` runs on the homepage. It measures clicks on navigation
+links, Order Now, and Seasonal Favorites cards (including their nested links).
+Normal link behavior is preserved. Each sample is in milliseconds and contains
+`kind`, a stable `name`, `startTime`, `duration`, and `method`.
+
+Read the latest 100 samples with `window.dailyGrindPerformance.getSamples()`.
+Subscribe to `window`'s `daily-grind:performance` custom event to consume
+`event.detail` in an analytics integration. No data is sent to a server.
+User Timing measures are also emitted, then cleared to bound buffer usage.
+
+When available, `event-timing` uses the browser's Event Timing duration from
+input timestamp through the next paint. Browsers only report events meeting
+their threshold (minimum 16ms), and round durations. Other clicks use
+`animation-frame-estimate`: two requestAnimationFrame callbacks cross a rendering
+boundary. This estimates visual response; it cannot certify physical display
+presentation, completion of CSS animations, or asynchronously loaded content.
+Pending measurements are discarded when the document is hidden or unloaded.
+Full-page destination loading is not measured by the originating homepage.
+
+The current page has no client-side router. History traversal and hash changes
+are automatically timed across a rendering boundary. For client-side view
+switches added later, wrap the update and await its DOM commit:
+
+```js
+await window.dailyGrindPerformance.measureViewSwitch('menu', async () => {
+  const data = await loadMenu();
+  await renderMenu(data); // Resolve after the DOM update is committed.
+});
+```
+
+Use stable view names without personal data. This API includes asynchronous
+work before the commit and the following rendering boundary; it preserves the
+update's result or rejection. Calling pushState/replaceState alone does not
+paint a view; wrap the actual render with this API. Automatic history/hash
+estimates cover synchronous handlers; use the API for asynchronous rendering.
+
+Run automated checks with `node --test tests/performance-monitor.test.cjs`.
